\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces Schematic representation of the third postulate what it means is that in a differential manifold we can always go from one coordinate chard to another.}}{17}{figure.caption.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.2}{\ignorespaces In the left there is the first chart $\phi _1$ which covers almost all the circumference but a point $p$ and to the right there is the other chard which covers almost all the circumference except a point $q$ diametrically opposite to $p$.}}{18}{figure.caption.4}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.3}{\ignorespaces Each point on the sphere has a small vicinity which maps as a disc on $\mathbb {R}^2$.}}{19}{figure.caption.5}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.4}{\ignorespaces Representation of traditional map from $S^2$ to $\mathbb {R}^2$ we can see there are multi valued points which wont allow us to cover the manifold $S^2$.}}{20}{figure.caption.6}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.5}{\ignorespaces Graphical representation to find teh stereographic projection of $^2$ to $\mathbb {R}^2$.}}{21}{figure.caption.7}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.6}{\ignorespaces Schematic representation of the map $f$, this takes elements from the manifold and maps then to the rect of the reals $\mathbb {R}$.}}{22}{figure.caption.8}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.7}{\ignorespaces The curve $\lambda $ maps from an open interval of the reals to the manifold, in the manifold this maps looks like, (remember this is just an scheme), a curve we see usually, from a point in that curve we can define a tangent vector $X_p$.}}{23}{figure.caption.9}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.8}{\ignorespaces Two tangent vectors $X_p$ and $Y_p$ which both are tangent to $p$ but live in two different curves, ($\lambda _1$,$\lambda _2$) but in the same vectorial space $T_p(M)$.}}{24}{figure.caption.10}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Graphical representation of the relative movement between the two test bodies.}}{40}{figure.caption.11}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces In the left there are two particles which are in a free fall to the earth an the inertial reference frame (IRF) is to the side of the earth, he observes a diagonal trajectory of the bodies. In the right we set the IRF inside the box with the two bodies, and he observes that there is a strange force which makes the bodies get together.}}{42}{figure.caption.12}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces In the left there is a light ray seeing from an inertial reference frame inside the box, and in the right there is the same ray but seeing from the earth.}}{43}{figure.caption.13}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.4}{\ignorespaces Space-time diagram representation for the Doppler effect analized before.}}{45}{figure.caption.14}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.5}{\ignorespaces The light cone for the space-time structure, in the $x$-axis there is $\mathbb {R}^3$ and in the $y$ axis there is the coordinate time $t$, a space-like vector is a vector which lives outwards the cone, a light-like lives on the border of the cone and a time-like lives inside the cone.}}{49}{figure.caption.15}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.6}{\ignorespaces Graphical representation of an integral time-like curve on a manifold $M$.}}{50}{figure.caption.16}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.7}{\ignorespaces The real time-like trajectory a particle will follow is $\lambda (\tau )$ and the other two will be little different trajectories but with initial an final points fixed.}}{51}{figure.caption.17}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.8}{\ignorespaces Affine parameterization of the geodesics, here we illustrate the path (geodesic) a free particle will follow which is parameterized with a parameter $t$ but this is some application of the other parameter $u$.}}{66}{figure.caption.18}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.9}{\ignorespaces Connection between $q$ and $p$ with a curve along a variation of $s$ with $X$ as tangent vector, in the same way $q$ and $r$ can be connected by a curve with $Y$ as tangent vector, $p$ and $u$ can be connected with a curve with $Y$ as tangent vector and same with $u$ and $r$ but with $X$ as tangent vector.}}{70}{figure.caption.19}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.10}{\ignorespaces Representation of all the possible subsets of $S$ constant and $t$ constant, this is an analogous to what is done in the formulation $3+1$ where we separate the space-time and folds as a Cauchy space. }}{79}{figure.caption.20}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.11}{\ignorespaces Representation of the region (manifold $M$) of integration and its surrounding surface$\Sigma M$.}}{88}{figure.caption.21}%
